\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{hs_nei_chesser()}: unbiased gene diversity (expected heterozygosity,
Hs) for one population at one locus, \verb{(n/(n-1)) * (1 - sum_p2 - ho/(2n))} (Nei & Chesser 1983). Unbiased at any FIS. Vectorised.
\item \code{hs_biallelic()}: the same at a biallelic site, from one allele's
frequency \code{p}.
\item \code{hs_stacks_pi()}: \verb{(2n/(2n-1)) * 2p(1-p)}, the estimator behind Stacks'
\code{Pi} column. Unbiased only when FIS = 0 and biased low otherwise;
provided so the two can be compared.
\item \code{gene_div_2n_counts()}: the same correction as \code{hs_stacks_pi()}, from
allele counts, so it also works at multi-allelic (haplotype) loci.
\item \code{hs_from_counts()}: \code{hs_nei_chesser()} from allele counts.
\item \code{fis_ratio_of_sums()}: \code{1 - sum(ho) / sum(he)} over loci -- a ratio of
sums, never a mean of per-locus ratios (Weir & Cockerham 1984). A locus
monomorphic in the population adds 0 to both sums, so including it
changes nothing.
\item \code{autosomal_het()}: heterozygosity per variant record rescaled to per
sequenced site (Schmidt et al. 2021), \code{het_per_snp * n_snps_used / n_sites_sequenced}; \code{NA} wherever \code{n_sites_sequenced} is missing or
smaller than \code{n_snps_used}. Vectorised, so each population can have its
own \code{n_sites_sequenced}.
\item \code{p_sampled()}: for each allele, the probability it appears at least once
in \code{g} gene copies drawn without replacement.
\item \code{rare_richness()}: rarefied allelic richness at one locus, the expected
number of distinct alleles in \code{g} gene copies (Hurlbert 1971; El
Mousadik & Petit 1996).
\item \code{rare_private()}, \code{rare_private_all()}: rarefied private allelic
richness, the expected number of alleles present in \code{g} copies from one
population and absent from \code{g} copies of every other population
(Kalinowski 2004; Szpiech et al. 2008).
}

The derivations are in the header of \code{R/estimators.R};
\code{\link[=diversity_core_selftest]{diversity_core_selftest()}} checks each estimator against brute-force
Monte Carlo and against published Stacks output.
